>However, consider the following YANI:
>
>Take a sokoban map with all boxes^H^H boulders on their solution squares.
>Now, imagine an agent able to *pull* single boulders in orthogonal
>directions (basically, the opposite of what Soko players can do).
>Turn this agent loose and let him pseudo-randomly drag boulders around
>for a bounded number of moves. Put the downstairs on the location of
>the agent when this movement ended.
>
>Presto, a sorta-random guaranteed-solvable sokoban level.
>
>Of course, the devil's in little details like: How do you come up
>with interesting map outlines (choose from a limited set)? How does
>the agent decide which boulder to drag next, and where?
>
>My suspicion is that you'd tend to end up with levels which were
>much easier than those hand-crafted by humans. But at least it would
>no longer be a "go to spoiler, perform listed moves in order" affair.
>
>Mimics could be used to good effect to even out the difficulty a bit.

  Most players don't seem to put much effort into solving the 
current Sokoban levels, because they're so difficult players use
spoilers (or configurable Sokoban games) to solve them.  


  So more-diverse but (much) easier Sokoban levels might actually
get players to put more effort into solving them, instead of 
going immediately to the spoilers.

  The down sides are:

  - Easier puzzles might not be long enough to result in 
    character strength gain from solving them.

  - Some players don't like solving Sokoban puzzles and prefer
    having a few puzzles with known solutions.  (Although why
    Nethack should suddenly start catering to its players is
    another question.)

  So emphasis on matching the current level of difficulty seems
misplaced to me, as it would still lead to using out-of-game
means to solve them.  Perhaps more levels could be substituted 
instead.  That would also help ensure enough boulder-pushing to
gain strength.

  For randomly generated Sokoban perhaps some way could be found 
for players to note especially good levels that are generated.

  

